// NEGATIVE TEST -- a program may not DEFINE a built-in name (N14-02).
//
// MEASURED BEFORE THIS REFUSAL EXISTED: this exact file compiled, linked, ran
// and exited 0. It printed `7` -- and the `7` did not come from the function
// below. The emitted C held BOTH of these:
//
//     long long print_int(long long x) { return x; }   // line 293
//     ...
//     __pd_print_int(7LL);                             // in main
//
// So the definition was real C that nothing could reach, and the call the
// author wrote went to the built-in instead. Nothing in the source says which
// of the two `print_int(7)` means; the compiler picked, and picked silently.
//
// A built-in cannot be replaced -- code generation maps every built-in name to
// `__pd_<name>` from the registry (src/builtins.rs), long after the type
// checker has forgotten there was a competing definition. So the choice is
// between a definition that is dead by construction and a refusal, and this is
// the refusal.
//
// THE OVER-REFUSAL CONTROL IS ELSEWHERE, and it has to be: this file stops at
// the first offending line, so a near-miss name written above it would prove
// nothing. `tests/03_functions_basic.pd` compiles and runs `print_int_of`,
// `printer` and a local `print_all` -- names that CONTAIN a built-in's name
// without being one.
fn print_int(x: i64) -> i64 {
    return x;
}

fn main() {
    print_int(7);
}
